<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Karel Doorman</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1889">1889</a>–<a href="/years/1942">1942</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Karel Doorman was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> rear admiral who commanded the combined Allied naval striking force in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">East Indies</a> in early 1942. On 27 February 1942 his fleet of Dutch, British, American and Australian ships was defeated by a Japanese force in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-java-sea">Battle of the Java Sea</a>, and he went down with his flagship, the cruiser De Ruyter. The Dutch navy later named ships after him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karel-doorman.jpg" alt="Karel Doorman" width="320" height="457" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ministerie van Defensie · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karel_Doorman_as_lieutenant-commander.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Karel Willem Frederik Marie Doorman was born in Utrecht on 23 April 1889 and raised as a Roman Catholic in a military family. He and his brother Lou were commissioned as midshipmen in 1906. In 1910 he became an officer and went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a> aboard the cruiser Tromp, and from January 1912 to December 1913 he served on survey vessels mapping the coastal waters of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-guinea">New Guinea</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1915 Doorman joined the Aviation Service and became one of the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> naval officers to earn pilot's wings. He trained pilots at Soesterberg and from 1918 at the Naval Air Base De Kooy in Den Helder, which he commanded from 1919 to 1921, and in 1922 he was made a Knight of the Order of Orange-Nassau for his work in organising naval aviation. Budget cuts and an arm injury ended his flying, and from 1921 to 1923 he attended the Higher Naval School in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a>.</p><p>He then served at sea and ashore: as gunnery officer and later first officer of De Zeven Provinciën, in command of the mine-layer Prins van Oranje and the destroyers Witte de With and Evertsen, and as chief of staff at Den Helder. In 1937 he went out as a captain to command the cruisers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, and in August 1938 he became Commander of Naval Aviation in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a>. He was promoted to rear admiral on 16 May 1940.</p><p>In early 1942 Doorman led the remnants of the ABDA Combined Striking Force. On 3 February Japanese bombers forced his ships back from Makassar, and on 19 February his attacks failed to stop the Japanese landing on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a> in the Battle of Badung Strait.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-java-sea">Battle of the Java Sea</a>, which lasted with interruptions for over seven hours, his outnumbered squadron was almost wholly destroyed. Following navy tradition, Doorman chose to go down with De Ruyter when a Japanese Long Lance torpedo sank her. He is remembered for the signal I am attacking, follow me, a loose rendering of his order All ships, follow me; one source doubts he said the words. He was posthumously made a Knight 3rd class of the Military William Order, and four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> navy ships have borne his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Karel Doorman” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the East Indies the Dutch fleet under <b>Karel Doorman</b> was destroyed in the Battle of the Java Sea in February 1942 and the colony surrendered to Japan in March.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Kingdom of the Netherlands · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Utrecht, in what is now <a href="/countries/netherlands">the Netherlands</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java-sea">Java Sea</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.09,5.12&amp;year=1923" aria-label="Utrecht on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4665 364 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Karel Doorman"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5115 634h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5115 634h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.09,5.12&amp;year=1923">What was here in 1923? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
